et planes  by now a celebrity the pilot was invited  to receive the diamonds to his knight's  cross at hitler's rastenberg  headquarters the wolf's lair during the  meeting hitler admitted to hartmann that  the war was lost what little hope was  left rested on the last ditch wonder  weapons developed by the third reich  weapons like the messerschmitt me262 the  early prototype of a jet fighter  luftwaffe general adolf galland asked  hotman to join the me262 test program  but the ace declined he preferred to  continue fighting with his unit jg 52  plus he had just earned 10-day license  which put good use by marrying ush on  the 10th of september by then hitler's  maniacal empire was crumbling besieged  on three fronts and yet jg 52 continued  fighting to the bitter end even after  the suicide of the fuhrer may the 8th  1945 was the last day of war in europe  but on that morning hostilities had not  ceased that's when over the skies of  bruno modern-day czech republic eric  shot down his 352nd plane a soviet yak-9  upon landing he learned that his  airfield was within range of incoming  soviet forces by now a major hartman  ordered his men to retreat westwood to  surrender to u.s infantry he felt that  they would be safer with the americans  than with the soviets what he did not  know was that according to the altar  agreement all their marked personnel  deployed on the eastern front would be  handed over to the red army and that is  exactly what happened on the 14th of may  as a pow of the soviets harma was  initially pressured to cooperate with  them by spying on fellow officers an  offer which he could and did refuse  earning him 10 days of solitary  confinement more harsh treatment  followed as nkpd interrogators tried to  extort information on the me262 program  harman did not cooperate and a  frustrated officer struck him with a  cane hartman replied in kind smashing a  chair onto the soviet's head several  months had passed and hartmann was being  held without charge these came only on  the 27th of december 1949 when soviet  authorities sentenced him to 20 years in  prison as a war criminal he was charged  with the killing of 780 soviet civilians  at briansk which he vehemently denied he  was also condemned for having destroyed  345 soviet aircrafts which of course he  did not deny but then again it was war  wasn't it harman was doomed to serve  years of hard labor in the hellish gulag  system unwilling to yield he refused to  perform his duties and was thrown again  into solitary his mistreatment led other  prisoners to organize a riot they even  managed to briefly overpower the guards  and free him from his cell they were  about to escape to freedom but hartmann  convinced them to stay put once beyond  the gates they all risked being shot on  site order was eventually restored  hartman took the occasion to file a  complaint with the commander of the camp  requesting an international inspection  and an appeal trial both requests landed  in the appropriate filing cabinet the  waste paper bin and the ace was  dispatched in the novichok gulag for a  five-month stint in confinement at this  point the soviet surprisingly accepted  to hold a new trial unsurprisingly they  confirmed the original sentence and sent  hartman to an even harsher camp on the  euro mountains luckily for him in 1955  the normalization of relationships  between the soviet union west germany  led to the release of the last german  pows eric hartman was one of them he was  finally able to return home to his wife  unfortunately he was never able to hold  in his arms their first born child eric  peter the boy had been born in 1945 and  subsequently died in 1948 while his  father was in captivity  [Music]  once reunited eric and ush could finally  lead a normal life they welcomed a  daughter ursula isabel on february 23  1957 and he was reinstated in the west  german air force as a commander of  fighter wing 71. the unit was initially  equipped with canadair sabres scheduled  to be replaced by the lockheed f-104  starfighters hartmann had had the  occasion to fly the starfighters during  a trip to the u.s while he admired their  performance he noted their frequent  engine problems and maintenance  difficulties overall he considered them  to be unsafe especially if flown by  inexperienced pilots he voiced his  concerns to an old friend general kim  herber inspector of the german air force  the general appeared sympathetic but  eventually cautioned hartmann from  talking about the subject to anyone else  the purchase of the starfighters was a  done deal a political decision taken by  defence minister strauss which overruled  the advice of experienced officers by  july 1960 west germany had become the  largest market for lockheed craft and  pilots had already begun training sadly  they also started dying over the span of  just five years 35 pilots perished in  non-combat missions by the early 1980s  when the f-104 was finally  decommissioned 116 pilots had lost their  lives harman's predictions had been  proven right but no one thanked him  quite the opposite his relentless  criticism had made many enemies within  the air force and the ministry when he  neglected to renew his pilot's license  due to an oversight his detractors took  the occasion to bring him to a military  tribunal on disciplinary charges hartman  was absolved but he was relieved of his  operational command and kicked upwards  to a desk job in cologne eventually  opted for an early retirement from the  military in september of 1970 working as  a civilian instructor until 1974. two  years later in february 1976 a u.s  senate subcommittee heard that lockheed  had been routinely bribing foreign  governments to secure contracts for  their f-104s 24 million 167 million  dollars in today's money had been  allocated by the corporation as bribe  money the scandal had mainly involved  the netherlands japan and italy but  according to a new york times article of  