reassess the situation if  it was favorable he would dive in for  another kill see decide attack break and  if possible attack again these tactics  are best exemplified by one of hartman's  most dangerous actions and the one that  got him closest to death ironically it  was due to friendly fire on august 19th  1943 hartman led a squadron of seven  fighters against a formation of 80  soviet fighter bombers dangerously close  to jg52 booby and his pilot soon sighted  their foes tailing in il-2 he let off  the burst of 20mm rounds scoring a  direct hit he then banked away sharply  lining his sides with the second catch  of the day another squeeze of the  trigger and another kill but this time  the ace flew too close to his downed foe  and debris from the explosions crashed  onto his engine suffering a mechanical  failure hartman crash-landed into a  field of sunflowers the down pilot was  reassured by the sight of a german truck  approaching only too late he realized  that the two soldiers on board were  soviets manning a captured vehicle  improvising harmon threw himself on the  ground feigning a leg injury his acting  was so convincing that the soviets  loaded him onto a stretcher and onto  their truck when the sound of incoming  german planes distracted the two  soldiers hartmann sprang to his feet  knocked out one of the guards and jumped  off the moving truck chased by the cries  and bullets of the fooled guards the  pilot was able to hide in the nearby  woods as darkness fell hartman's  compatriots back at jg 52 were restless  they knew he had been downed but still  they hoped that he could be rescued one  man in particular could not stand just  wait by and see this was bilbao mertens  chief of hartman's ground crew more  importantly he was eric's best friend  against all orders bimmel grabbed a  rifle and set off on a one-man mission  beyond enemy lines to rescue his bro  unbeknownst to him hartman was on his  way back under the cover of darkness he  followed a soviet recon patrol guessing  that they would be heading towards the  german lines after a two-hour trek a  loud cry startled him halton it was a  german century but before the pilot  could state his identity the soldier  fired a shot the bullet tore through  eric's trousers very very close to his  jewels miraculously he was unharmed  hartmann quickly shouted i'm a german  pilot for god's sake let me come through  the sentry was not convinced yet fearing  hartman to be a soviet imposter it was  only after long and intense questioning  that eric's identity was confirmed and  he was driven back to jg52 that morning  mertens returned to base two he'd lost  all hope of finding his best friend and  could not believe that it made it back  alive this was one wartime bromance that  didn't end in tragedy  in september 1943 at the age of just 21  eric hartman was promoted to captain and  continued with his relentless streak of  successes on the 20th of the month he  recorded his 100th aerial victory by the  end of october his tally had reached 148  and he was awarded the knight's cross of  the iron cross by the end of february  1944 close to 200 aircraft had fallen to  boobies bullets soviet pilots now feared  the distinctive markings of his bf-109  red heart bearing the nickname of his  sweetheart oosh and a black tulip-shaped  motif painted on the engine cover it was  this black marking that earned hartman  the nickname of black devil of the south  soviet commanders even placed a 10 000  ruble bounty on the devil's head but  soviet pilots preferred to immediately  disengage upon noticing the black tulip  unwilling to scare off his prey so  easily hartmann adopted a plain colour  scheme more victories and more awards  followed in march 1944 hartmann his four  memento kopinski and two more aces  barkhorn and vice were summoned by the  fuhrer himself to receive the knight's  cross of the iron cross with oak leaves  according to eric the four pilots showed  up completely drunk and hartman was told  off by an officer as he wore hitler's  hat by mistake hartman walked away from  the meeting with the impression that  hitler was an unimpressive boring and  disturbed individual most of the pilots  in his unit shared the same view and in  general considered all nationalist  socialist idiocy  a little sickening harman agreed with  his commanding officer commodore hrabak  when he warned new pilots that if they  thought they were fighting for national  socialism and the fuhrer they needed to  transfer to the waffen-ss they were  fighting a war against a superb enemy  that superb enemy was by that time  winning the war when eric and friends  returned to the east the german defenses  were collapsing in may of 1944 jg 52 was  relocated to romania where they would be  employed in the defense of the  strategically vital ploy estee oil  fields it was in this theater that  hartman faced the us army air forces for  the first time on the 24th of june the  black devil claimed the first victory  against a p-51 mustang later in the  month he shot down his second mustang  but he didn't have time to celebrate his  eight american fighters gave chase  harman dodged their bullets in a series  of evasive maneuvers until he ran out of  fuel with no other option he released  the canopy of his bf-109 and bailed out  with his parachute as he was floating  down a p-51 approached menacingly at  speed at the last moment it banked away  sharply the american pilots and the  downed german ace saluted each other a  sign of respect among knights of the sky  if the american had known that he had  the black devil in his sights maybe he  would have decided otherwise but hartman  lived another day to fight on on august  17 1944 he officially became the top  scoring ace of all time when he downed  his 274th plane one week later he  surpassed the 300 mark by performing his  most astonishing feat on a single day he  flew two missions during which time he  shot down a total of 11 soviet planes  by now a celebrity the pilot was invited  to receive the diamonds to his knight's  cross at hitler's rastenberg  headquarters the wolf's lair during the  meeting hitler admitted to hartmann that  the war was lost what little hope was  left rested on the last ditch wonder  weapons developed by the third reich  weapons like the messerschmitt me262 the  early prototype of a jet fighter  luftwaffe general adolf galland asked  hotman to join the me262 test program  but the ace declined he preferred to  continue fighting with his unit jg 52  plus he had just earned 10-day license  which put good use by marrying ush on  the 10th of september by then hitler's  maniacal empire was crumbling besieged  on three fronts and yet jg 52 continued  fighting to the bitter end even after  the suicide of the fuhrer may the 8th  1945 was the last day of war in europe  but on that morning hostilities had not  ceased that's when over the skies of  bruno modern-day czech republic eric  shot down his 352nd plane a soviet yak-9  upon landing he learned that his  airfield was within range of incoming  soviet forces by now a major hartman  ordered his men to retreat westwood to  surrender to u.s infantry he felt that  they would be safer with the americans  than with the soviets what he did not  know was that according to the altar  agreement all their marked personnel  deployed on the eastern front would be  handed over to the red army and that is  exactly what happened on the 14th of may  as a pow of the soviets harma was  initially pressured to cooperate with  them by spying on fellow officers an  offer which he could and did refuse  earning him 10 days of solitary  confinement more harsh treatment  followed as nkpd interrogators tried to  extort information on the me262 program  harman did not cooperate and a  frustrated officer struck him with a  cane hartman replied in kind smashing a  chair onto the soviet's head several  months had passed and hartmann was being  held without charge these came only on  the 27th of december 1949 when soviet  authorities sentenced him to 20 years in  prison as a war criminal he was charged  with the killing of 780 soviet civilians  at briansk which he vehemently denied he  was also condemned for having destroyed  345 soviet aircrafts which of course he  did not deny but then again it was war  wasn't it harman was doomed to serve  years of hard labor in the hellish gulag  system unwilling to yield he refused to  perform his duties and was thrown again  into solitary his mistreatment led other  prisoners to organize a riot they even  managed to briefly overpower the guards  and free him from his cell they were  about to escape to freedom but hartmann  convinced them to stay put once beyond  the gates they all risked being shot on  site order was eventually restored  hartman took the occasion to file a  complaint with the commander of the camp  requesting an international inspection  and an appeal trial both requests landed  in the appropriate filing cabinet the  waste paper bin and the ace was  dispatched in the novichok gulag for a  five-month stint in confinement at this  point the soviet surprisingly accepted  to hold a new trial unsurprisingly they  confirmed the original sentence and sent  hartman to an even harsher camp on the  euro mountains luckily for him in 1955  the normalization of relationships  between the soviet union west germany  led to the release of the last german  pows eric hartman was one of them he was  finally able to return home to his wife  unfortunately he was never able to hold  in his arms their first born child eric  peter the boy had been born in 1945 and  subsequently died in 1948 while his  father was in captivity  [Music]  once reunited eric and ush could finally  lead a normal life they welcomed a  daughter ursula isabel on february 23  1957 and he was reinstated in the west  german air force as a commander of  fighter wing 71. the unit was initially  equipped with canadair sabres scheduled  to be replaced by the lockheed f-104  starfighters hartmann had had the  occasion to fly the starfighters during  a trip to the u.s while he admired their  performance he noted their frequent  engine problems and maintenance  difficulties overall he considered them  to be unsafe especially if flown by  inexperienced pilots he voiced his  concerns to an old friend general kim  herber inspector of the german air force  the general appeared sympathetic but  eventually cautioned hartmann from  talking about the subject to anyone else  the purchase of the starfighters was a  done deal a political decision taken by  defence minister strauss which overruled  the advice of experienced officers by  july 1960 west germany had become the  largest market for lockheed craft and  pilots had already begun training sadly  they also started dying over the span of  just five years 35 pilots perished in  non-combat missions by the early 1980s  when the f-104 was finally  decommissioned 116 pilots had lost their  lives harman's predictions had been  proven right but no one thanked him  quite the opposite his relentless  criticism had made many enemies within  the air force and the ministry when he  neglected to renew his pilot's license  due to an oversight his detractors took  the occasion to bring him to a military  tribunal on disciplinary charges hartman  was absolved but he was relieved of his  operational command and kicked upwards  to a desk job in cologne eventually  opted for an early retirement from the  military in september of 1970 working as  a civilian instructor until 1974. two  years later in february 1976 a u.s  senate subcommittee heard that lockheed  had been routinely bribing foreign  governments to secure contracts for  their f-104s 24 million 167 million  dollars in today's money had been  allocated by the corporation as bribe  money the scandal had mainly involved  the netherlands japan and italy but  according to a new york times article of  september 1976 german defense minister  her strauss had been receiving kickbacks  to the tune of 12 million dollars from  lockheed as early as 1961 but it was too  late to undo what was arguably eric  hartman's greatest defeat even the ace  pilot who had never been shot down by  enemy fire could not triumph against the  machinations of the military-industrial  complex hartman spent his last years in  relative obscurity occasionally being  interviewed and celebrated by his old  foes the usaf he died of a heart  condition on the 20th of september 1993  at the age of 71 in veal imstornbc four  years later the russian chief military  prosecutor posthumously acquitted him of  all charges as an alleged war criminal  [Music]  you
 

